Here is a simple React component that provides autocomplete functionality without relying on any third-party packages:
```
import React, { useState, useEffect } from 'react';

const Autocomplete = ({ options, onSelect, initialValue }) => {
  const [inputValue, setInputValue] = useState(initialValue || '');
  const [suggestions, setSuggestions] = useState([]);
  const [query, setQuery] = useState('');

  useEffect(() => {
    const handleInput = (event) => {
      const value = event.target.value;
      setQuery(value);
      const suggestions = options.filter((option) => option.toLowerCase().includes(value.toLowerCase()));
      setSuggestions(suggestions);
    };

    document.addEventListener('input', handleInput);

    return () => {
      document.removeEventListener('input', handleInput);
    };
  }, [options]);

  const handleSelect = (option) => {
    setInputValue(option);
    onSelect(option);
  };

  const renderSuggestions = () => {
    return suggestions.map((suggestion) => (
      <div key={suggestion} className="suggestion" onClick={() => handleSelect(suggestion)}>
        {suggestion}
      </div>
    ));
  };

  return (
    <div className="autocomplete">
      <input
        type="text"
        value={inputValue}
        onChange={(event) => setInputValue(event.target.value)}
        placeholder="Type to autocomplete..."
      />
      <ul className="suggestions-list">
        {suggestions.length > 0 && (
          <li className="suggestions-header">Suggestions:</li>
        )}
        {renderSuggestions()}
      </ul>
    </div>
  );
};

export default Autocomplete;
```
Here's an explanation of how the component works:

* We use the `useState` hook to store the input value, suggestions, and query string.
* We use the `useEffect` hook to set up an event listener for the input element. When the user types something, we filter the options array to find suggestions that match the user's input, and update the `suggestions` state accordingly.
* We render a list of suggestions below the input element, using the `renderSuggestions` function. Each suggestion is a div element that contains the suggestion text, and has an `onClick` event handler that calls the `handleSelect` function when clicked.
* When the user selects a suggestion, we update the input value and call the `onSelect` function with the selected suggestion.

